{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{9}{4},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-333347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{9}{4}y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)((1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{9}{4}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.6$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-333347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-333347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-333347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and IV only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "III and IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-333347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-5Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.25.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)((1-5Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-5Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-5Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-5Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-5Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <5Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-332530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category"