 when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}-4({y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}+16{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+8{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}<Δx-\\frac{1}{4} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-333930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-333930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-333930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II, III, and IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-333930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.95$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+2.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3.3}| \\\\  & =0.3030…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-334255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "IV only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, III, and IV"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.6$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{5}{8}| \\\\  & =0.625<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.6)| & =|1-1.6+\\frac{(1.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{25}| \\\\  & =0.68<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.2)| & =|1-2.2+\\frac{(2.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{61}{50}| \\\\  & =1.22>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.5)| & =|1-3.5+\\frac{(3.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{29}{8}| \\\\  & =3.625>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the Modified Euler Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-334715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and IV only"] ["text" "II, III, and IV only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334715" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{233}{384}| \\\\  & =0.606…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|1-1.5+\\frac{(1.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{35}{128}| \\\\  & =0.273…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.5)| & =|1-2.5+\\frac{(2.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{83}{128}| \\\\  & =0.648…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3.2+\\frac{(3.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3427}{1875}| \\\\  & =1.827…<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-334431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334431" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.25$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.25)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.25}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.25}| \\\\  & =0.8<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =0.5<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.5}| \\\\  & =0.4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(9)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+9}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{10}| \\\\  & =0.1<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-332737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-332737" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, III, and IV"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.1$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.4$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|1-0.1+\\frac{(0.1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{72\\,387}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.9048…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|1-1.2+\\frac{(1.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{199}{625}| \\\\  & =0.3184<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|1-2.9+\\frac{(2.9)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.9)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.9)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{284\\,921}{240\\,000}| \\\\  & =1.1871…>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.4)| & =|1-3.4+\\frac{(3.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11\\,987}{5000}| \\\\  & =2.3974>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9242b54191e1e856695fa36b86a53a4550ce90c5b1d3bf3df374a038352e5aee"} {"question" "q-334436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "q-334436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/state.json" "file_sha256" "a26952bffafeed167b27b7f062955a1b9ee203f7724c890567461f6238fee480"} {"question" "e-21636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), \\, y(0) = 8, \\ x \\geq 0, \\, \\lambda = 6,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as $y_{n+1} = \\left(1 - 6\\Delta x\\right)^n y_n.$\n\n- The method is stable for $|1 - 6\\Delta x| < 1.$\n\n- The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.1.$\n\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $\\Delta x = 0.2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21636.json" "file_sha256" "95491f3b02ad90223857ce140241361bc60b3a0b97a295685302c8cff9651682"} {"question" "e-21636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method $y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$ is stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 8, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -6y, \\qquad y(0) = 8.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n,y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-6{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-6{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-6Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-6Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-6Δx)((1-6Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-6Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-6Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =8{(1-6Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-6Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-6Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <6Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $\\Delta x = 0.1$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $\\Delta x = 0.2$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21636.json" "file_sha256" "95491f3b02ad90223857ce140241361bc60b3a0b97a295685302c8cff9651682"} {"question" "e-21638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda = 1$ is the parameter of the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda \\Delta x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21638.json" "file_sha256" "95459e6f1b8f17c09953966cca93cf3c2eb60d9954225ebc848053d7048a3c20"} {"question" "e-21638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method $y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$is stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda=1,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <Δx<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$ satisfies the inequality, and\n\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler's method is stable for all given step sizes.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21638.json" "file_sha256" "95459e6f1b8f17c09953966cca93cf3c2eb60d9954225ebc848053d7048a3c20"} {"question" "e-21637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation $y'=-\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0, \\lambda = 12,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2.$\n\n- The stability region is $\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac16\\right)^2\\right| < 1.$\n\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n\n- The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21637.json" "file_sha256" "fdd394bd439d102fdecc38a472892c62924d34b253c4ad7e217be0589b69fbbe"} {"question" "e-21637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method  $y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$ is stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -12y, \\qquad y(0) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-12{y}_{n}-12({y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-24{y}_{n}+144{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-12{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+72{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-12Δx+72(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(12Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-12Δx+72(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |72{(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac1{12}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac12\\right| < 1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72{(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 72{(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{12})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{12}<Δx-\\frac{1}{12} & <\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15,$ and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21637.json" "file_sha256" "fdd394bd439d102fdecc38a472892c62924d34b253c4ad7e217be0589b69fbbe"} {"question" "e-21639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following values is the Runge–Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$\n\n- $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x)\n= 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}\n  - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6}\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21639.json" "file_sha256" "5398c45c11e7bdddb7866319a82ec81e4dbe0d62aed74780028110075a205492"} {"question" "e-21639" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A numerical method\n$y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x)\n= 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}\n  - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6}\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.6)| & =|1-0.6+\\frac{(0.6)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.6)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.6)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{2747}{5000}| \\\\  & =0.5494<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.4)| & =|1-1.4+\\frac{(1.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4241}{15000}| \\\\  & =0.282…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.8)| & =|1-2.8+\\frac{(2.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{639}{625}| \\\\  & =1.0224>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.3)| & =|1-3.3+\\frac{(3.3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{167747}{80000}| \\\\  & =2.096…>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots,$ so it is not absolutely stable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049825/example-21639.json" "file_sha256" "5398c45c11e7bdddb7866319a82ec81e4dbe0d62aed74780028110075a205492"}] "usage" {"e-21636" [] "e-21639" [] "e-21637" [] "q-332530" [] "q-332533" [] "q-332737" [] "e-21638" []} "basis" 1740 "retractions" [[17592186229588 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 5$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The update formula can be written as $y_{n + 1} = (1 - 5Δx)^{n}y_{n}$.\n2. The method is stable for $\\mid 1 - 5Δx \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is stable for step size $Δx = 0.2$.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 0.25$."] [17592186229591 :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $λ$ is the parameter of the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ > 0$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. $λΔx = 0.67$\n2. $λΔx = 1.20$\n3. $λΔx = 1.80$\n4. $λΔx = 2.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(λΔx) = 1 - λΔx$."] [17592186229600 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $λ$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $λΔx = 0.25$\n2. $λΔx = 1$\n3. $λΔx = 1.5$\n4. $λΔx = 9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n$R(λΔ) = \\frac{1}{1 + λΔx}$."] [17592186228855 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), \\, y(0) = 8, \\ x \\geq 0, \\, \\lambda = 6$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. The update formula can be written as $y_{n+1} = \\left(1 - 6\\Delta x\\right)^n y_n$.\n2. The method is stable for $\\mid 1 - 6\\Delta x \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.1$.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$."] [17592186228855 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 8, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -6y, \\qquad y(0) = 8\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nEuler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n,y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n$y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + (-6y_{n}) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 6y_{n} \\cdot Δx; = (1 - 6Δx)y_{n}$.\nHence, statement I is false.\n- For stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Here, we have\n$y_{n}|= (1 - 6Δx)y_{n - 1}; = (1 - 6Δx)((1 - 6Δx)y_{n - 2}); = (1 - 6Δx)^{2}y_{n - 2}; ⋮⋮; = (1 - 6Δx)^{n}y_{0}; = 8(1 - 6Δx)^{n}$.\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1$, as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n- Expanding the inequality $\\left|1 - 6\\Delta x\\right| < 1$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & < 1 - 6Δx < 1 \\\\ -2 & < - 6Δx < 0 \\\\ 0 & < 6Δx < 2 \\\\ 0 & < Δx < \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:Since $\\Delta x = 0.1$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.Since $\\Delta x = 0.2$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [17592186228857 :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda = 1$ is the parameter of the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. $\\Delta x = 0.5$\n2. $\\Delta x = 0.9$\n3. $\\Delta x = 1.2$\n4. $\\Delta x = 1.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$."] [17592186228857 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\mid R(λΔx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - λΔx \\mid \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n$0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$.\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$.\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda=1$, this region simplifies to\n$0 \\mid < Δx < 2$.\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $\\Delta x = 0.5$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 0.9$ satisfies the inequality,\n- $\\Delta x = 1.2$ satisfies the inequality, and\n- $\\Delta x = 1.8$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler's method is stable for all given step sizes.\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\"."] [17592186228856 :question/problem "For the initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0$, the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$Δy_{n}^{(p)}|= f(x_{n}, y_{n}) \\cdot Δx,; y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(f(x_{n}, y_{n}) + f(x_{n + 1}, y_{n} + Δy_{n}^{(p)})) \\cdot Δx$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation $y'=-\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0, \\lambda = 12$, and step size $\\Delta x$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2$.\n2. The stability region is $\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac16\\right)^2\\right| < 1$.\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n$."] [17592186228856 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$, just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $\\mid R(\\lambda\\Delta x) \\mid < 1$, is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -12y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n$y_{n} + Δy_{n}^{(p)}|= y_{n} + f(x_{n}, y_{n}) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx$\nThus,\n$y_{n + 1}|= y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(f(x_{n}, y_{n}) + f(x_{n + 1}, y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx)) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(-12y_{n} - 12(y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx)) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} + \\frac{1}{2}(-24y_{n} + 144y_{n} \\cdot Δx) \\cdot Δx; = y_{n} - 12y_{n} \\cdot Δx + 72y_{n} \\cdot (Δx)^{2}; = [1 - 12Δx + 72(Δx)^{2}]y_{n}$.\nSo, the stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2$. Hence, statement I is true.\n- The method is stable when\n$\\mid R(12Δx) \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 1 - 12Δx + 72(Δx)^{2} \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid 72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid \\mid < 1$.\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac1{12}\\right)^2 + \\dfrac12\\right| < 1$.\nHence, statement II is false.\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} & < 1 \\\\ 72(Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} & < \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (Δx - \\frac{1}{12})^{2} & < \\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{12} < Δx - \\frac{1}{12} & < \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ 0 < Δx & < \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15$, and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."] [17592186228858 :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge–Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$\n2. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$\n3. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$\n4. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n$R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$."] [17592186228858 :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n$$\n\nis stable for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\la